{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon EKS and Amazon EKS Distro now supports Kubernetes version 1.33 - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) and Amazon EKS Distro now support Kubernetes version 1.33. This version includes new features such as stable support for sidecar containers, topology-aware routing and traffic distribution, consideration of taints and tolerations for pod topology spread constraints, user namespaces within Linux pods, dynamic resource allocation for network interfaces, and in-place resource resizing for vertical scaling of pods","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/05/amazon-eks-distro-kubernetes-version-1-33/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon EKS and Amazon EKS Distro now supports Kubernetes version 1.34 - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) and Amazon EKS Distro now support Kubernetes version 1.34. Users can create new EKS clusters using version 1.34 and upgrade existing clusters through the EKS console, eksctl command line interface, or infrastructure-as-code tools. Kubernetes version 1.34 introduces several new features and bug fixes, including projected service account tokens for improved security, Pod-level resource requests and limits for simplified resource management, and Dynamic Resource Allocation (DRA) prioritized alternatives for improved resource scheduling. EKS now supports Kubernetes version 1.34 in all regions where EKS is available, and EKS Distro builds of Kubernetes version 1.34 are available through ECR Public Gallery and GitHub. To learn more about the changes in Kubernetes version 1.34 and instructions to update clusters, visit EKS documentation. EKS cluster insights can be used to check for potential issues impacting cluster upgrades","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/10/amazon-eks-distro-kubernetes-version-1-34/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon EKS and Amazon EKS Distro now supports Kubernetes version 1.36 - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) and Amazon EKS Distro now support Kubernetes version 1.36. Users can create new EKS clusters using version 1.36 and upgrade existing clusters through the EKS console, eksctl command line interface, or infrastructure-as-code tools. Kubernetes version 1.36 introduces several new features and bug fixes, including the promotion of User Namespaces to general availability for unprivileged container root mappings, Mutating Admission Policies for CEL-based resource mutations, In-Place Pod-Level Resources Vertical Scaling, and Resource Health Status reporting. These features are available in all AWS Regions where EKS is available, including AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. EKS cluster insights can help identify potential issues during upgrades. EKS Distro builds of Kubernetes version 1.36 are available through ECR Public Gallery and GitHub. EKS version lifecycle policies are detailed in the documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/06/amazon-eks-distro-kubernetes-version-1-36/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"amazon-ec2-image-builder","skill_description":"Creates and automates custom image builds with EC2 Image Builder - Linux, Windows, and macOS AMIs, and container images to ECR. Covers the build IAM role, Amazon-managed and custom components, image recipes, infrastructure and distribution configuration (launch templates, SSM parameters, other Regions), one-off builds, recurring scheduled pipelines for golden AMI automation and OS patching, custom image workflows, and diagnosing failed builds. Applies when creating, automating, or scheduling AMI or container image builds with Image Builder, or when debugging a failed build. Not for launching instances from existing AMIs, AMI lifecycle/retirement, or general EC2 fleet management.\n\nServices: imagebuilder, ec2, ecr, iam\nTasks: create, configure, deploy, debug\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: compute, containers","skill_name":"amazon-ec2-image-builder"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-containers","skill_description":"Builds, deploys, debugs, and optimizes containerized workloads on Amazon EKS, ECS, Fargate, ECR, and Elastic Beanstalk. Covers EKS operations and add-ons; ECS task definitions, services, Exec, Express Mode, Managed Instances, and Action Logs; ECR repositories and lifecycle policies; and Elastic Beanstalk platforms plus Beanstalk Cluster Mode for running containerized applications on service-managed EKS compute. Use for container deployment, service selection, configuration, scaling, health, troubleshooting, image workflows, or deciding between Beanstalk-managed Cluster Mode and direct EKS. Should be used instead of relying on internal knowledge for these services.\n\nServices: ecs, ecr, eks, apprunner, beanstalk\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: containers, compute, kubernetes","skill_name":"aws-containers"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify AWS docs on AL2 AMI support for EKS 1.33"}

{"id": "e79526a5-16c6-41c7-a35b-47c562fa9278", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "Confirmed — that's almost certainly your root cause. AL2 AMI support ends at Kubernetes 1.32; EKS does not release AL2 AMIs for 1.33+, and standard AL2 AMI support officially ended November 26, 2025. If your new node group's launch template is still pointing at an AL2 AMI/AMI type while the cluster is on 1.33, that AMI is version-mismatched with the control plane — a very common cause of `NodeCreationFailure` (kubelet/bootstrap incompatibility, nodes never register, group fails before reaching desired capacity).

Let me pull the actual node group config and failure details to confirm this is what's happening in your account rather than assuming.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_U3pVgIu2dC7ZNytlKGERj5", "tool_name": "gather_context", "input": {"prompt": "{
  "task": "Diagnose why a new EKS managed node group fails to join a cluster upgraded to Kubernetes 1.33, failing with NodeCreationFailure, while the old 1.32 node group works fine.",
  "context": "User upgraded an EKS cluster from 1.32 to 1.33. They created a new node group using an AL2 (Amazon Linux 2) AMI/AMI type. Nodes fail with NodeCreationFailure and never join the cluster. The old 1.32 node group (presumably still AL2 or AL2 launch template) works fine. Strong hypothesis: AL2 AMIs are not released/supported for Kubernetes 1.33+ (last supported version is 1.32, EOL Nov 26 2025), so the node group's AMI type or launch template AMI is incompatible with the 1.33 control plane, causing bootstrap/kubelet version mismatch and nodes never registering.",
  "resources": ["EKS clusters in this account/region", "their node groups", "associated launch templates and AMI IDs/types"],
  "timeframe": "recent — focus on current state and most recent node group creation/failure events",
  "criteria": "1) Identify the EKS cluster(s) running 1.33. 2) For each, list node groups, their amiType/launch template AMI, and Kubernetes version compatibility. 3) Pull the specific NodeCreationFailure error message/reason from the node group status or associated Auto Scaling Group activity/EC2 instance status to confirm root cause (AMI version mismatch, bootstrap failure, kubelet version skew, or something else like IAM/networking). 4) Compare the old working 1.32 node group's AMI type against the new failing one to highlight the difference. 5) Report exact AMI IDs/types, error messages, and timestamps found."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Check EKS node group config and NodeCreationFailure details"}], "created_at": null}

