uster Mode for running containerized applications on service-managed EKS compute. Use for container deployment, service selection, configuration, scaling, health, troubleshooting, image workflows, or deciding between Beanstalk-managed Cluster Mode and direct EKS. Should be used instead of relying on internal knowledge for these services.\n\nServices: ecs, ecr, eks, apprunner, beanstalk\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: containers, compute, kubernetes","skill_name":"aws-containers"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"Amazon EKS and Amazon EKS Distro now support Kubernetes version 1.37 - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) and Amazon EKS Distro now support Kubernetes version 1.37. Users can create new EKS clusters using version 1.37 and upgrade existing clusters through the EKS console, eksctl command line interface, or infrastructure-as-code tools. Kubernetes version 1.37 introduces several improvements, including the Metrics API promotion to general availability, Dynamic Resource Allocation (DRA) device taints and tolerations graduation to general availability, and Horizontal Pod Autoscaler scale-to-zero graduation to beta. The Metrics API provides Pod and node CPU and memory usage for Horizontal Pod Autoscaler and kubectl top. DRA drivers and administrators can taint devices to avoid scheduling unless tolerated. Horizontal Pod Autoscaler now scales workloads to zero Pods when idle and back up when demand returns. Kubernetes version 1.37 is available in all AWS Regions where EKS is available, including AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. Users can check for upgrade issues using EKS cluster insights and find EKS Distro builds of Kubernetes version 1.37 in ECR Public Gallery and GitHub. EKS version lifecycle policies are also documented","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/amazon-eks-distro-kubernetes-version-1-37/"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify AWS docs on AL2 EKS AMI support for Kubernetes 1.33", "parent_id": 